{"custom_id": "f5d3fd3e85d35726a9106467ddfff99021c8c29e26f8c226", "meta": {"loop": "topk4L10", "task": "infer_discrepancy_patterns_4", "occurrence": 0, "attempt": 0, "max_tokens": 128000, "prompt_sha256": "b60c944a389aa7f362cf9f5066850295818e0dbf2c792c886774152d1a0cda6c", "prompt_chars": 10727}, "batch_id": "msgbatch_012SJ24TPGRkepNH2RjwH6y4", "saved_utc": "2026-10-23T10:11:31+00:00", "message": {"id": "msg_011CgJz4vigaBopQxrp1oNtC", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "I'm going through the discrepancies between gold labels and LLM output: phenotype descriptions like \"abnormal retinal vascular development,\" molecular deficiencies like \"deficiency of norrin,\" and symptoms like \"spasticity\" are all labeled DiseaseClass in gold but tagged O by the LLM, suggesting the gold standard treats broader biological/clinical descriptors as disease-related even when not literal disease names.\n\nThe pattern seems to be that the LLM correctly identifies named diseases and recognized symptom terms (like \"mental retardation,\" which it got right as DiseaseClass) but misses generic disease-manifestation phrases embedded in descriptive clinical prose, since those don't match a typical disease-naming pattern it's trained to recognize.\n\nLooking at the contrast more carefully: SpecificDisease TPs are all named entities with acronyms, Modifier TPs use disease terms attributively, but the discrepancies are all descriptions of disease manifestations or pathological states rather than named entities. So the boolean feature is whether the span denotes a symptom/phenotypic condition rather than a proper nosological name.\n\nI'm testing whether this holds — \"mental retardation\" is also phenotypic yet was correctly tagged, so maybe the real trigger is the presence of a nominalized abstract process or state noun (deficiency, development, spasticity) describing a pathological condition, rather than a disease name itself. Checking: \"abnormal retinal vascular development\" uses a process nominalization, \"deficiency of norrin\" uses a state nominalization — both fit, while \"mental retardation\" is a fixed disease-like term that doesn't.\n\nBut then I notice \"deficient activity of fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase\" is structurally similar yet left unannotated in the gold data, which undercuts a fully deterministic rule — the gold labeling itself seems inconsistent here, so I'm considering whether the rule needs tighter scoping around which state nominalizations count.\n\nLooking at another document, I see the same pattern repeating: \"abnormal retinal vascular development\" gets labeled DiseaseClass but \"retinal telangiectasis,\" a comparable lesion descriptor in the same sentence, doesn't. This confirms the gold annotations are inconsistent about tagging pathophysiological descriptors versus disease-like lesion terms, which I should flag as a recurring discrepancy pattern rather than a one-off error.\n\nThe underlying trigger seems to be whether the head noun belongs to a disease lexicon (like \"syndrome,\" \"disorder,\" \"dysplasia,\" or \"retardation\") versus being a generic process nominalization (like \"deficiency,\" \"development,\" \"spasticity\") — the model tends to label O when the head noun isn't a recognizable diagnostic term, even within a \"characterized by\" or \"secondary to\" clinical framing. I'm trying to pin down a single boolean feature that explains the split: whether the head noun is a lexicalized clinical diagnosis term versus a generic state/process word.\n\nSo \"mental retardation\" gets matched because \"retardation\" is a nosological label, while \"spasticity\" and \"deficiency of norrin\" in the same text go unlabeled because they're symptom or deficit nouns, not disease names — and crucially these are in the same sentence, same syntactic position, so positional and syntactic features don't separate them, only the semantic nature of the head noun does. Comparing further, \"G6PD deficiency\" gets tagged SpecificDisease because the enzyme-name prefix makes the whole phrase a recognized disease label, whereas \"deficiency of norrin\" is just a generic molecular deficit without that recognized disease status — a useful contrast for the rule.\n\nI'm also noting that gold annotation itself is inconsistent here, since things like \"deficient activity of FALDH\" or \"retinal telangiectasis\" follow the same descriptive pattern but aren't labeled, so whatever rule I propose won't be exhaustive. I'm drafting the final pattern insight: name it something like \"Phenotypic/Pathological-State Descriptors Labeled as DiseaseClass,\" describe the confusion trigger as non-nosological heads (symptoms, deficits, abnormal-process nominalizations) appearing in disease-characterization clauses that the model reads as descriptive prose rather than entity mentions, support it with the mental-retardation-vs-spasticity and G6PD-deficiency-vs-deficiency-of-norrin contrasts, and propose a rule: tag DiseaseClass when the head noun denotes a pathological state/symptom/process predicated of a disease, but not when the head is itself a named disease or a bare molecular entity.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "## Step 1: Deep Dive Analysis\n\n**Contrast:**\n- **Discrepancies (all Gold = DiseaseClass):** \"abnormal retinal vascular development,\" \"deficiency of norrin,\" and \"spasticity.\" Each names a clinical manifestation, a pathological process, or a molecular deficit described in prose. None has a nosological head such as *disease*, *syndrome*, or *disorder*, and none is an eponym or acronym.\n- **True Positives:** SpecificDisease spans are named diseases (eponyms, acronyms, \"G6PD deficiency\"). Modifier spans are disease nouns used attributively (\"ovarian cancer\"). The only DiseaseClass TP, \"mental retardation,\" comes from the same sentence as the missed \"spasticity.\"\n- **Separating feature:** a **semantic/lexical** one. The question is whether the head names a condition as a nosological label or describes a symptom, state, or process. Positional and syntactic features do not separate the cases. \"Mental retardation\" and \"spasticity\" share the same list, position, and frame.\n\n**Isolate Trigger:** The model appears to treat DiseaseClass as requiring a recognized disease label. Symptom nouns, deficit nominalizations (\"deficiency of X\"), and abnormal-process nominalizations (\"abnormal X development\") read as ordinary descriptive prose. The clause frames (\"is characterized by,\" \"results in,\" \"secondary to\") make these spans look like explanations of the disease rather than entity mentions.\n\n**Hypothesis Check:**\n- *Spasticity* (single-word symptom noun) fits.\n- *Deficiency of norrin* (state nominalization with an \"of\" complement) fits.\n- *Abnormal retinal vascular development* (process nominalization with adjectival modifiers) fits.\n- *Mental retardation* is the exception the model got right. It is a lexicalized diagnostic label, which supports the head-type explanation.\n- **Caveat:** The gold standard is inconsistent. \"Deficient activity of FALDH,\" \"retinal telangiectasis,\" and \"exudative retinal detachment\" follow the same pattern but are unlabeled. The rule captures a systematic tendency, not every gold instance.\n\n## Step 2: Pattern Insight\n\n- **Pattern Name:** Phenotypic and Pathological-State Descriptors Tagged as DiseaseClass\n\n- **Confusion Trigger:** Non-nosological head nouns (symptoms, deficits, and abnormal-process nominalizations such as *spasticity*, *deficiency of [protein]*, *abnormal [tissue] development*) appearing in disease-characterization clauses. The model reads these as descriptive prose rather than condition mentions and assigns O.\n\n- **Contrastive Evidence:** The DiseaseClass TP \"mental retardation\" has a head (\"retardation\") that names a condition as a diagnostic label. \"G6PD deficiency\" (SpecificDisease TP) is a named disease because it is a standard condition label. \"Deficiency of norrin\" (FN) is a generic deficit of a gene product with no standard disease name. The TP spans have nosological heads, while the FN spans have state or process heads.\n\n- **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Tag a span as **DiseaseClass** when both conditions hold:\n  1. Its head denotes a pathological condition, symptom, or abnormal state/process (for example, *-ity* symptom nouns, *deficiency/deficient*, or *abnormal* + process noun), and\n  2. It is predicated of a disease, either in a disease-description clause (\"X is characterized by Y,\" \"Y results in Z,\" \"Y is secondary to Z\") or in a coordinated feature list alongside a named disease.\n\n  Do **not** tag it when the head is a named disease, eponym, acronym, or a \"disease/syndrome/disorder\" label, which should be SpecificDisease, or when the span is only a gene or protein name without a pathological-state head.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4197, "output_tokens": 7758, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 6442}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}